---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const trialsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Phase I: safety (~20–100 people)', note: 'First human exposure — healthy volunteers, escalating doses, watching for toxicity. Only ~70% pass. The dose for later phases is chosen here.' },
    { math: '2 · Phase II: does it work? (~100–500)', note: 'First efficacy signal in patients — response rates, dosing confirmation. ~33% pass. The attrition cliff: most drugs die here.' },
    { math: '3 · Phase III: does it beat the standard? (1,000–5,000+)', note: 'Randomised, controlled, often blinded, multi-site. The comparison against current care — the trial that decides approval. ~25–30% pass.' },
    { math: '4 · Phase IV: the real world reports in', note: 'After approval, surveillance catches rare effects (too rare for trial sizes), long-term outcomes and misuse. Vioxx\'s 2004 withdrawal — after years on the market — is the lesson that approval is not the finish line.' },
  ],
};

const evidenceQuiz = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: 'A trial of a new drug recruits only patients who volunteer enthusiastically, and the control arm gets the old drug during a hospital renovation that disrupted care. The new drug looks better. What has broken?',
      choices: ['Randomisation', 'Blinding', 'The placebo effect', 'Intention-to-treat'],
      answer: 0,
      why: 'Both problems are failures of randomisation\'s core guarantee: that arms differ only by treatment. Selection put different people in each arm, and the period effect contaminated controls — no statistical adjustment reliably repairs a broken randomisation.',
    },
    {
      q: 'A drug cuts a 10-year death risk from 20% to 15%. What is the number needed to treat (NNT)?',
      choices: ['5', '20', '33', '100'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Absolute risk reduction is 20% − 15% = 5%. NNT = 1 ÷ 0.05 = 20 patients treated for ten years to prevent one death. (The 25% relative reduction is the same fact, but NNT makes the clinical weight visible.)',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why can a Phase III trial involving thousands of patients still miss a serious side effect?',
      choices: ['Trials do not record side effects', 'The effect may be rarer than the trial can detect or appear only after years', 'Regulators suppress the data', 'Patients hide symptoms'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'To reliably detect an adverse event occurring in 1 in 10,000, you need tens of thousands of exposed patients plus follow-up time — beyond almost any Phase III. This is precisely what Phase IV pharmacovigilance exists for.',
    },
    {
      q: 'A paper reports a strong benefit in the "over-65 female diabetic" subgroup — one of 24 subgroups reported. Why be sceptical?',
      choices: ['Subgroups are never valid', 'With many subgroups, some will show significance by chance — was it pre-specified?', 'Diabetes invalidates trials', 'Older patients are outliers'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Test 24 independent subgroups at p of 0.05 and roughly one will be "significant" by luck alone. The claim is credible only if pre-registered, biologically motivated, and consistent across trials — post-hoc subgroup findings are hypothesis-generating, not practice-changing.',
    },
  ],
};

const ctBiasMatch = {
  "prompt": "Blinding guards three choke points — pair each bias with its mechanism:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "expectation effects",
      "right": "patients report differently when they know what they received"
    },
    {
      "left": "performance bias",
      "right": "clinicians treat and measure differently"
    },
    {
      "left": "detection bias",
      "right": "outcome assessors code events differently"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The full architecture adds allocation concealment and intention-to-treat — analyse everyone as randomised, because cleaning up the groups re-imports the confounding randomisation removed."
};

const ctHrtCompare = {
  "prompt": "One therapy, two study designs, two answers — drag between them:",
  "before": {
    "label": "the observational cohorts",
    "text": "People who took the treatment were healthier in every measurable way, and the association looked strongly protective — decades of consistent data."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "the randomised trial",
    "text": "Randomise the same question (the Women's Health Initiative) and the answer reverses: harm. The pioneers and the healthier takers were selection, not effect."
  },
  "why": "Confounding does not shrink with sample size — the observational-versus-randomised reversal is evidence-based medicine's recurring lesson."
};

const ctConfoundSpot = {
  "prompt": "A note on reading associations — one line is wrong. Click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Consistency across many datasets is genuinely reassuring.",
    "But confounding does not shrink with sample size.",
    "So a vast, consistent association can be treated as settled — the bigger the effect, the safer the inference.",
    "Only randomisation balances the confounders you did not measure.",
    "An observational effect, however large, remains a hypothesis until a randomised comparison settles it."
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "Magnitude and consistency are not cures for confounding: a large observational association stays a hypothesis until a randomised comparison settles it — no statistical adjustment reaches the unmeasured.",
  "why": "The entire value of randomisation is that it balances what you did not think to measure."
};

const ctPowerEstimate = {
  "prompt": "Rule of thumb: n per arm ≈ 16 × p(1−p) ÷ δ², at 80% power. Control event rate 20%, hoped reduction to 15% (δ = 0.05). Roughly how many patients per arm?",
  "unit": "patients per arm",
  "answer": 1024,
  "factor": 1.6,
  "why": "16 × 0.2 × 0.8 ÷ 0.0025 ≈ 1,024 per arm — over 2,000 patients and years of recruitment. Shrink the detectable difference to δ = 0.02 and the demand grows to ~6,400 per arm.",
  "context": "Which is why \"no significant difference\" in a 40-patient study means almost nothing — absence of evidence is not evidence of absence."
};

const ctEndpointBuckets = {
  "prompt": "What you measure is what you know — file each measure:",
  "buckets": [
    "clinical endpoint",
    "surrogate endpoint"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "death",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "stroke",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "fracture",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "a cholesterol value",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "tumour shrinkage on imaging",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "suppressed ectopic beats — the measure CAST debunked",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "Surrogates are acceptable only when validated — shown repeatedly to track the clinical endpoint in this drug class, this population. CAST is what decoupling costs."
};

const ctPaperOrder = {
  "prompt": "Reading a paper critically, in the order the checks usually break — drag them into place:",
  "items": [
    "Was it registered, and were the endpoints pre-specified?",
    "Is the comparison fair — active control, adequately dosed comparator?",
    "Intention-to-treat analysis, everyone as randomised?",
    "Are the subgroups pre-specified?",
    "Are harms reported with the same energy as benefits?"
  ],
  "why": "Outcome switching, manufactured superiority, re-imported selection bias, chance subgroups, quiet harms — the CONSORT checklist exists because each failure recurred often enough to need a name."
};

const ctVaccineJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "The vaccine programme's speed came from lowering the evidentiary bar.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "The logic was skipped nowhere — funding removed the usual serial pauses, and the epidemic's scale supplied endpoints quickly."
    },
    {
      "s": "The pivotal efficacy trials enrolled 30,000–45,000 participants with event-driven analysis.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Unprecedented Phase III size, read out in under a year."
    },
    {
      "s": "Real-world evidence later showed strong persistent protection against severe disease even as effectiveness against infection waned with variants.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Endpoints, surrogates, generalisability and pharmacovigilance — the chapter's whole vocabulary, at once."
    }
  ]
};

const ctDesignBranch = {
  "root": "Choose the architecture. Two scenarios, two designs.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "An epidemic is moving faster than serial trials can answer. What runs?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "A platform trial — adaptive, multi-arm, evidence-driven",
          "next": "platform",
          "feedback": "The infrastructure that answered early."
        },
        {
          "label": "The classic two-arm trial, one question at a time",
          "next": "classic",
          "feedback": "Gold standard for a single question — but the epidemic outpaces serial pauses."
        }
      ]
    },
    "platform": {
      "text": "One protocol, arms entering and leaving as evidence accumulates, tens of thousands of patients, Bayesian interim analyses — the RECOVERY model.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Now the biosimilar question",
          "next": "bio"
        }
      ]
    },
    "classic": {
      "text": "Rigorous, readable — and it answers one question in the time an adaptive infrastructure answers five.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Continue",
          "next": "bio"
        }
      ]
    },
    "bio": {
      "text": "A biosimilar cannot ethically be tested against placebo: the non-inferiority design asks \"is the new drug not-worse?\" — read the margin, not the p-value.",
      "choices": []
    }
  },
  "why": "Different questions demand different architectures — and each design's limits are written in what it cannot ask."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/advanced/clinical-trials-and-evidence">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Medicine. It builds on the foundational lesson The Heart
    and Circulation and the intermediate lessons Public Health &amp; Epidemiology,
    Pharmacology Basics and How Doctors Think: Diagnosis — epidemiology supplies the
    population thinking, pharmacology the drug-development frame, and clinical reasoning the
    habit of asking how we know. The sibling advanced lesson Cancer Biology &amp; Oncology
    supplies this lesson's favourite worked examples.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why randomisation is the only design that balances unknown confounders;</li>
    <li>compute power-driven sample sizes and the number needed to treat, and say what each depends on;</li>
    <li>trace a drug through the four phases and state where candidates die;</li>
    <li>judge whether an endpoint is clinical or a surrogate — and whether the surrogate was ever validated;</li>
    <li>read a paper critically: registration, blinding, intention-to-treat, subgroups, harms, publication bias.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Medicine's central epistemic problem: patients get better for reasons that have nothing to do
    with the treatment. Diseases resolve spontaneously; symptoms fluctuate and regression to the mean
    guarantees that people seek care at their worst; the act of being treated (attention, ritual,
    expectation) has real physiological effects. Before randomisation, medicine was a graveyard of
    therapies that "obviously worked" — bloodletting among them — because experience without control
    cannot separate treatment effect from natural history. The randomised controlled trial is the
    design that solves this, and everything in this chapter is either how it works, how it scales
    into a drug-development pipeline, or how to read one honestly.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four phases and the attrition between them" widget="equationStep" config={trialsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards sketch a funnel, and the percentages
    between them are the story — most candidates clear Phase I, roughly a third clear
    Phase II (the attrition cliff), and a quarter to a third clear Phase III. Notice what
    each phase is actually asking — safe, active, better than the standard, still safe out
    in the market — and that the dose chosen in Phase I is inherited by everything
    downstream.
  </p>

  <h2>Randomisation: why it is the load-bearing wall</h2>
  <p>
    The problem any comparison faces is confounding — patients who receive the new treatment differ
    systematically from those who do not (they are sicker, or richer, or treated at better centres),
    and those differences, not the drug, may drive the outcome. Randomisation severs the link: if
    allocation is truly by chance, then <em>in expectation</em> the arms are identical on every
    variable — measured or not, known or not. That is the magic: no other design (matching,
    adjustment, propensity scores) balances the unknown confounders, because you cannot adjust for
    what you did not measure. Blinding then protects the comparison at its other choke points:
    patients report differently when they know (expectation effects), clinicians treat and measure
    differently (performance bias), and outcome assessors code events differently (detection bias).
    The full architecture is allocation concealment (the randomisation sequence hidden from enrolment
    decisions), double-blinding, and intention-to-treat analysis — analyse everyone as randomised,
    including dropouts and protocol violators, because the moment you clean up the groups you
    re-import the confounding randomisation removed.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each bias with the choke point blinding guards" widget="match" config={ctBiasMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example of the failure mode.</strong> Suppose a surgical innovation is adopted by
    enthusiastic pioneers, while control patients receive conventional care during a period when a
    hospital renovation disrupted wards. Non-randomised comparison: new surgery, 80% survival;
    conventional, 65%. Randomise (REPAIR-like trials of this era) and the gap collapses or reverses
    — the pioneers selected healthier candidates, and the renovation period selected sicker ones.
    History is rich with these reversals: hormone replacement therapy looked cardioprotective in
    observational cohorts (women who took it were healthier in every measurable way) and showed harm
    in the Women's Health Initiative randomised trial. The observational-versus-randomised gap is
    the recurring object lesson of evidence-based medicine.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="drag between the cohort and the randomised answer" widget="compare" config={ctHrtCompare} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a large, consistent observational association is nearly
    as good as a trial, especially when the effect looks big." It seems reasonable — decades
    of data feel stronger than one experiment. But confounding does not shrink with sample
    size: women who took hormone replacement therapy were healthier in every measurable way,
    and the association looked strongly protective until the Women's Health Initiative
    randomised the question and found harm. Only randomisation balances the confounders you
    did not measure; an observational effect, however large and however consistent, remains
    a hypothesis until a randomised comparison settles it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="one line of the inference note over-trusts size" widget="spot-error" config={ctConfoundSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> randomisation, blinding and intention-to-treat analysis are the
    load-bearing walls of any fair comparison. Now watch the logic scaled into a decade-long
    pipeline with brutal attrition.
  </p>

  <h2>The pipeline: phases and their arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    Drug development is a funnel with brutal attrition: from roughly 5,000 screened compounds, one
    reaches approval; the process takes 10–15 years and costs on the order of a billion dollars
    (estimates range 0.7–2.6 billion including failures). Phase I establishes human safety and dose
    (healthy volunteers, escalation rules, pharmacokinetics); Phase II asks for an efficacy signal in
    patients and kills most candidates — the attrition cliff; Phase III is the confirmatory gamble,
    large, multi-site, powered for the approval decision; Phase IV is the market as laboratory. Each
    phase's design inherits the previous phase's uncertainty: an under-dosed Phase III kills a good
    drug, an over-dosed one poisons it — dose selection is where trials most often fail.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Power, computed.</strong> How big must Phase III be? The rough rule for a dichotomous
    endpoint: n per arm ≈ 16 × p(1−p) ÷ δ², where p is the control-event rate and δ the absolute
    improvement worth detecting, with 80% power at p = 0.05. Control mortality 20%, hoped reduction
    to 15% (δ = 0.05): n ≈ 16 × 0.2 × 0.8 ÷ 0.0025 = 1,024 per arm — over 2,000 patients, years of
    recruitment. Try to detect δ = 0.02 and the requirement grows to ~6,400 per arm. This arithmetic
    is why trials are powered for the <em>sponsor's</em> question, and why "no significant
    difference" in a 40-patient study means almost nothing: absence of evidence is not evidence of
    absence, and the confidence interval is the honest way to say what the study could and could not
    exclude.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the sample size before the calculator does" widget="estimate" config={ctPowerEstimate} />

  <h2>Endpoints: what you measure is what you know</h2>
  <p>
    Clinical endpoints (death, stroke, fracture) are gold but slow and expensive; surrogate endpoints
    (a cholesterol value, tumour shrinkage, a biomarker) are fast and measurable. The recurring
    disaster of therapeutics is surrogates that decoupled: anti-arrhythmics suppressed ectopic beats
    (the surrogate) and increased mortality (the outcome) — the CAST trial's finding killed a
    standard practice that had been confidently treating for years. Surrogates are acceptable only
    when validated — shown repeatedly to track the clinical endpoint in this drug class, this
    population. Modern accelerated-approval pathways deliberately trade on this: conditional approval
    on surrogate response rates, with confirmatory trials (increasingly, with withdrawal teeth)
    required afterwards. Know which endpoint a headline is using before believing it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="file each measure: clinical endpoint or surrogate?" widget="sort-buckets" config={ctEndpointBuckets} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "statistically significant" means clinically important. A huge
    trial can find a statistically robust effect of trivial size (relative risk reduction 3% on a
    0.4% baseline event rate); a small trial can find an enormous effect that fails p = 0.05. The
    professional reading reports <strong>absolute risk reduction</strong> and the
    <strong>number needed to treat</strong>: a statin cutting 10-year risk from 20% to 15% (ARR 5%)
    gives NNT = 20; the same relative benefit in a 2%-risk patient gives ARR 0.5% and NNT = 200.
    Same drug, same trial, different patient — the entire art of individualised prescribing is this
    recomputation.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a paper critically</h2>
  <p>
    The checklist, in the order it usually breaks. <strong>Was it registered, and were the endpoints
    pre-specified?</strong> Outcome switching — reporting the endpoint that worked after seeing the
    data — was common enough that registries (clinicaltrials.gov, mandated for publication in major
    journals) exist to catch it; compare registered primary outcome to reported one. <strong>Is the
    comparison fair?</strong> Active controls beat placebos for ethics, but an under-dosed comparator
    manufactures superiority. <strong>Intention-to-treat?</strong> Per-protocol analyses re-import
    selection bias. <strong>Are the subgroups pre-specified?</strong> Twenty-four reported subgroups
    guarantee one "significant" finding at p = 0.05 by chance alone. <strong>Are harms reported with
    the same energy as benefits?</strong> Trials systematically under-report adverse events. The
    CONSORT checklist exists because each of these failures recurred often enough to need a name.
    Then the highest-level move: place the result in the evidence hierarchy — systematic reviews and
    meta-analyses of pre-registered randomised trials sit at the summit, with heterogeneity (I²)
    telling you whether studies agree enough to pool, and funnel-plot asymmetry or registry-comparison
    studies exposing <strong>publication bias</strong> — the systematic disappearance of negative
    trials, quantified for antidepressants (turnaround study: FDA registry data nearly halved the
    published effect size).
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="rebuild the critical-reading sequence" widget="order" config={ctPaperOrder} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "if it passed peer review in a good journal, the finding
    is settled." Peer review checks a manuscript's coherence, not its data. This section's
    own evidence says otherwise: outcome switching replaced failed primary endpoints with
    post-hoc ones, unpre-specified subgroups manufactured significance by chance, and
    registry comparison showed published antidepressant trials nearly halving the effect
    sizes in the FDA's own records. Publication is a claim entering a debate — check the
    registration, the pre-specified endpoint and the harms table before letting a headline
    change what you believe.
  </p>

  <h2>The regulatory layer: approval and after</h2>
  <p>
    Trials exist inside a regulatory architecture. Regulators (FDA, EMA, MHRA and
    counterparts) define evidentiary standards: Phase III typically requires adequate
    well-controlled evidence, pre-specified endpoints, and benefit that justifies risk for
    the <em>indicated population</em> — approvals are narrow for a reason, and off-label use
    is the medical system's risk transfer. Accelerated pathways (conditional approval,
    breakthrough designation, priority review) trade surrogate endpoints for speed with
    confirmatory obligations; the accelerated-approval withdrawal mechanism — finally
    exercised meaningfully in the 2020s (an Alzheimer's drug's marketing withdrawn, several
    oncology indications retracted after failed confirmatory trials) — is the system's
    self-correction being tested in public. <strong>Pharmacovigilance</strong> is the
    permanent phase: spontaneous-report systems (where under-reporting and reporting bias
    live), mandated registries (pregnancy exposure, long-term safety), periodic safety
    updates, and signal-detection algorithms scanning databases for patterns. The regulatory
    story is also political economy: user fees fund reviewers, trial obligations shape where
    trials run (increasingly global, with ethics questions about populations that bear risk
    without assured access), and patent timelines (the 20-year clock runs from filing, so
    effective market exclusivity is much shorter) shape which trials get run at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> approval is a gate, not a finish line — pharmacovigilance
    watches the market the way trials watched the ward. Next: the whole pipeline compressed
    into the fastest modern case on record.
  </p>

  <h2>Vaccines: the pipeline's fastest modern case</h2>
  <p>
    The COVID-19 vaccine programme compressed the standard sequence without skipping its
    logic: Phase I safety (mRNA platforms' first human doses in 2020), Phase II
    immunogenicity and dose selection, and Phase III efficacy trials of unprecedented size
    (30,000–45,000 participants, event-driven analysis) read out in under a year — possible
    because funding removed the usual serial pauses and the epidemic's scale supplied
    endpoints quickly, not because the evidentiary bar moved. The trial results (efficacy
    around 90% against symptomatic disease in the initial window) and the subsequent
    real-world evidence (strong persistent protection against severe disease, waning
    effectiveness against infection as variants emerged) are the best modern teaching case
    for this entire chapter: endpoints, surrogates, generalisability and pharmacovigilance,
    all at once.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — the fastest pipeline, read honestly" widget="judge" config={ctVaccineJudge} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: why does intention-to-treat analysis protect the comparison that randomisation bought?</li>
    <li>Application: a trial reports a 25% relative risk reduction on a baseline 10-year risk of 4%. Compute the absolute risk reduction and the number needed to treat, and say which framing a patient should hear.</li>
    <li>Why can a surrogate endpoint be worse than no endpoint at all?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Analysing everyone as randomised keeps the arms
    comparable; cleaning up the groups re-imports exactly the selection confounding
    randomisation removed, since dropouts and protocol violators are not random. (2) The
    absolute risk reduction is a quarter of 4%, namely 1%; the number needed to treat is
    100 patients for ten years to prevent one event. The patient should hear the absolute
    framing: the same relative benefit on a 20% baseline gives NNT 20 — the two
    prescriptions are not equally worthwhile. (3) When the surrogate decouples from the
    clinical outcome it stands in for: anti-arrhythmic drugs suppressed ectopic beats (the
    surrogate) while increasing death (the outcome) in CAST — treating the number rather
    than the patient, with only the number validated.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Randomisation is the load-bearing wall: the only mechanism that balances unknown confounders.</li>
    <li>Blinding and intention-to-treat protect the comparison at its remaining choke points.</li>
    <li>The pipeline is a funnel: Phase I safety and dose, Phase II the attrition cliff, Phase III the confirmatory gamble, Phase IV the market as laboratory.</li>
    <li>Power arithmetic decides what a study can detect; absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.</li>
    <li>Surrogate endpoints count only when validated — CAST shows what happens when they decouple.</li>
    <li>Report absolute risk reduction and NNT: statistical significance is not clinical importance.</li>
    <li>Registration, pre-specified endpoints and harms tables are the reader's defence against outcome switching and publication bias.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling advanced lessons put the evidence habit to work: Cancer Biology &amp;
    Oncology contains the screening trials whose biases you have now learned to detect,
    Cardiovascular Pathophysiology runs the same molecular-to-population arc for heart
    disease, and Medical Imaging shows how the measurements that become endpoints are
    produced.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Designs beyond the classic RCT.</strong> The RECOVERY platform trial (COVID era) showed
    what adaptive, multi-arm infrastructure can do: one protocol, many treatment arms entering and
    leaving as evidence accumulates, tens of thousands of patients, and life-saving answers
    (dexamethasone) in months instead of years. Adaptive designs, Bayesian interim analyses and
    master-protocols are now mainstream; equivalence and non-inferiority designs (asking "is the new
    drug not-worse," for when a biosimilar cannot ethically be tested against placebo) require
    reading the margin, not the p-value. <strong>Fraud and replication.</strong> The Surgisphere
    scandal (2020) — retracted Lancet and NEJM papers built on a database that could not exist —
    forced journal-level reform and is the modern case study in why data sharing matters; retired
    classics like Wakefield's autism fraud show the social cost of evidence failure.
    <strong>The generalisability problem.</strong> Trials exclude exactly the patients clinicians
    treat: the elderly, multi-morbid, pregnant, and polypharmacy-adjacent — median trial participants
    remain younger, whiter and male-skewed relative to disease populations, and regulators increasingly
    demand diversity action plans. Real-world evidence (claims data, registries) can extend
    conclusions but re-opens the confounding door — its honest use is hypothesis plus calibration
    against trials. <strong>Open questions:</strong> how to run trials during epidemics at the speed
    pathogens move; whether peer review adds measurable quality; and whether AI-drafted protocols and
    synthetic control arms (borrowing historical patient data as comparators) will shrink trials
    faster than they import bias. Evidence-based medicine, it turns out, needs evidence about itself.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="walk the design decision" widget="branch" config={ctDesignBranch} />
  <p>
    Health economics completes the decision architecture. The randomised trial answers "does
    it work?"; the cost-effectiveness analysis answers "is it worth it?" — quality-adjusted
    life-years (QALYs) gained per pound, with thresholds (historically around £20,000–30,000
    per QALY at NICE) rationing implicitly. The arithmetic is consequential and contested:
    QALYs weight years by quality, which can disadvantage disabled populations (a documented
    equity critique), and the thresholds are administrative conventions, not laws of nature.
    The fuller frame — cost per life saved, value-of-statistical-life estimates, discounting
    of future health — makes explicit what every health system decides implicitly. And the
    access question closes the loop back to trials: pricing negotiations use the trial
    evidence, so trial design (which comparator, which population, which endpoint) ultimately
    shapes who gets the drug. The advanced reading of any clinical paper now includes its
    economic evaluation — because approval, reimbursement and access are one pipeline with
    different gates. The lesson generalises beyond medicine: any evidence-based policy
    eventually meets a budget, and the meeting is where the evidence's real-world meaning is
    set.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: randomisation, NNT, power, subgroup traps" widget="quiz" config={evidenceQuiz} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> commit to an answer before reading the explanation.
    The four questions walk this chapter's spine — broken randomisation, NNT arithmetic,
    why rare harms escape even large trials, and subgroup traps — and the explanations
    matter more than the scores: each one names the design principle the wrong options
    quietly violate. Missed the NNT question? Recompute the absolute risk reduction first —
    that ordering is the habit.
  </p>

